- Cellist Pablo Casals (1876–1973) is widely credited with reviving interest in Johann Sebastian Bach''s Six Suites for Unaccompanied Cello.
- Before Casals, these suites were generally treated as technical exercises rather than concert repertoire.
- Casals first encountered the sheet music in a secondhand shop in Barcelona when he was thirteen years old.
- He spent twelve years studying the suites privately before performing them in public.
- His 1936–1939 studio recordings of all six suites became some of the best-selling classical recordings of the twentieth century.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Casals discovered Bach's Six Suites for Unaccompanied Cello in a Barcelona secondhand shop at age thirteen and spent the next twelve years studying them privately before he felt ready to perform them publicly, as described in the passage."},{"key":"B","text":"Once dismissed as mere technical exercises, Bach's Six Suites for Unaccompanied Cello became celebrated concert repertoire after Pablo Casals championed them through performances and landmark studio recordings."},{"key":"C","text":"Pablo Casals, who lived from 1876 to 1973, is remembered as one of the most influential cellists in history, largely because of his best-selling recordings of Bach's Six Suites for Unaccompanied Cello."},{"key":"D","text":"Bach's Six Suites for Unaccompanied Cello, which Casals recorded in a series of studio sessions between 1936 and 1939, went on to become some of the best-selling classical recordings of the twentieth century."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'The correct choice is the sentence that describes the suites as "once dismissed as mere technical exercises" that "became celebrated concert repertoire" after Casals championed them, because it explicitly contrasts the suites'' status before and after Casals''s involvement, fulfilling the stated rhetorical goal. The sentence about discovering the music in a Barcelona shop and studying it for twelve years narrates Casals''s personal history with the suites but does not contrast how the suites were regarded in the broader musical world. The sentence identifying Casals as one of the most influential cellists in history focuses on his reputation rather than presenting a before-and-after contrast in how the suites were perceived. The sentence about the 1936–1939 recording sessions and their commercial success describes an outcome of Casals''s work but does not establish the contrast with the suites'' earlier dismissal as exercises.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-10-11"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-10-11","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);



Text 2: Wildlife manager Jorge Saldana has noted that while wolf reintroduction programs can yield measurable ecological improvements, such programs also impose significant costs on livestock producers in adjacent agricultural areas, costs that must be addressed through compensation schemes to maintain community support.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"By conceding the ecological advantages while stressing the economic costs such programs impose on livestock producers in nearby areas"},{"key":"B","text":"By disputing Lindgren's claim that wolves were historically present in the regions under consideration and arguing that reintroduction therefore lacks ecological justification"},{"key":"C","text":"By arguing that deer overpopulation should be managed through expanded hunting permits rather than predator reintroduction"},{"key":"D","text":"By questioning the scientific methodology Lindgren used to link wolf reintroduction to riparian vegetation regrowth"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'The correct answer is the choice about conceding ecological advantages while stressing economic costs. Text 2 explicitly states that wolf reintroduction "can yield measurable ecological improvements" but also imposes costs on livestock producers, meaning Saldana acknowledges the benefits Lindgren describes while raising an additional concern. The choice about disputing historical wolf presence is unsupported because Saldana does not challenge the premise that wolves were historically present. The choice about managing deer through hunting permits introduces a proposal found nowhere in either text. The choice about questioning Lindgren''s scientific methodology is unsupported because Saldana does not raise concerns about how Lindgren''s conclusions were derived.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 60, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-10-11"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-10-11","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);






Text 2: In contrast, researcher Pavel Dragovic argues that the letter format in these same novels served a subversive purpose: by granting fictional correspondents private, unmonitored voices, authors could explore taboo subjects and dissenting viewpoints that would have been censored in more overtly public literary forms.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Dragovic interprets the letter format as enabling transgression against social norms, whereas Khoury interprets it as reinforcing those norms"},{"key":"B","text":"Dragovic focuses on the commercial success of epistolary novels in the eighteenth-century literary marketplace, whereas Khoury focuses on how those novels were received by contemporary literary critics"},{"key":"C","text":"Dragovic argues that epistolary novels declined in popularity because of their increasingly controversial subject matter, whereas Khoury argues that the genre thrived precisely because it avoided controversy"},{"key":"D","text":"Dragovic claims that epistolary novelists intentionally obscured their authorship to evade government censorship, whereas Khoury claims that those same authors actively sought public recognition for their moral contributions to society"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'The correct answer is the choice stating that Dragovic interprets the letter format as enabling transgression while Khoury interprets it as reinforcing norms. Khoury argues the novels modeled virtuous behavior and guided readers toward socially sanctioned conduct, meaning the format reinforced prevailing norms. Dragovic argues the format allowed authors to explore taboo subjects and dissenting viewpoints, meaning it enabled transgression against those same norms. The choice about commercial success and critical reception introduces topics neither scholar discusses. The choice about declining popularity due to controversial content goes beyond what either text states; neither discusses the genre''s popularity trajectory. The choice about obscuring authorship and seeking public recognition attributes specific claims about author identity and public recognition that appear nowhere in either text.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-10-11"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-10-11","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);


- The baobab tree (Adansonia digitata) is native to sub-Saharan Africa and can live for over one thousand years.
- Its trunk can store up to 120,000 liters of water, which helps the tree survive long dry seasons.
- Communities in Madagascar, Senegal, and Zimbabwe use baobab fruit to make nutrient-rich beverages and porridges.
- The fruit pulp contains six times the vitamin C of an orange and twice the calcium of milk by weight.
- International demand for baobab fruit powder has grown steadily since the European Union approved it as a food ingredient in 2008.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"The baobab tree, which is native to sub-Saharan Africa and can live for over a thousand years, stores up to 120,000 liters of water in its trunk to survive dry seasons."},{"key":"B","text":"Rich in essential nutrients, baobab fruit pulp contains six times the vitamin C of an orange and twice the calcium of milk by weight, making it a valuable dietary resource."},{"key":"C","text":"Since the European Union approved baobab fruit powder as a food ingredient in 2008, international demand for the product has increased steadily."},{"key":"D","text":"Communities across sub-Saharan Africa, including those in Madagascar, Senegal, and Zimbabwe, have long used baobab fruit to prepare nutrient-rich beverages and traditional porridges."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'The correct choice is the sentence stating that baobab fruit pulp contains six times the vitamin C of an orange and twice the calcium of milk, because it directly emphasizes the nutritional value of the fruit by citing specific comparative nutrient data. The sentence about the baobab tree''s lifespan and water storage focuses on the tree''s biological adaptations, not the fruit''s nutritional content. The sentence about European Union approval and international demand addresses commercial history rather than nutritional value. The sentence about communities in Madagascar, Senegal, and Zimbabwe mentions nutrient-rich beverages but describes cultural uses rather than specifying what makes the fruit nutritionally valuable — it only partially addresses the stated goal without providing the concrete nutritional evidence that would emphasize value.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 75, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-10-11"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-10-11","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);










Text 2: A recent large-scale study led by neuroscientist Erik Hedlund found no statistically significant difference in executive function between bilingual and monolingual adults after controlling for socioeconomic status, educational attainment, and frequency of cognitively demanding leisure activities.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"It challenges Obasi's conclusion by suggesting that the cognitive advantages attributed to bilingualism may reflect confounding variables"},{"key":"B","text":"It reinforces Obasi's research by independently confirming that bilingual individuals exhibit stronger executive function than monolingual peers across all socioeconomic and educational backgrounds"},{"key":"C","text":"It extends Obasi's work by identifying the specific neurological mechanisms responsible for the enhanced cognitive flexibility observed in bilingual individuals"},{"key":"D","text":"It replicates Obasi's findings using a substantially larger and more demographically diverse sample and thereby strengthens the overall case for bilingualism conferring measurable cognitive benefits"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'The correct answer is the choice stating that Hedlund''s finding challenges Obasi''s conclusion by suggesting confounding variables. Hedlund found no significant difference in executive function after controlling for socioeconomic status, education, and leisure activities, which implies that the advantages Obasi observed may have been driven by those confounders rather than by bilingualism itself. The choice claiming Hedlund reinforces Obasi contradicts Text 2, which reports no significant difference rather than confirmation of an advantage. The choice about identifying neurological mechanisms is unsupported because Text 2 describes a behavioral comparison, not a neurological investigation. The choice about replicating Obasi''s findings misrepresents Hedlund''s results, which contradict rather than confirm the bilingual advantage Obasi reported.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"evidence_mismatch"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"evidence_mismatch"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 75, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-10-11"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-10-11","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);


- Architect Lina Bo Bardi (1914–1992) emigrated from Italy to Brazil in 1946.
- Her design for the São Paulo Museum of Art (MASP), completed in 1968, suspended the main exhibition hall above an open civic plaza using two massive concrete beams.
- The open plaza beneath the museum was intended to serve as a public gathering space accessible to all residents, not just museum visitors.
- Most art museums of the era enclosed their ground floors with walls, limiting public access to ticketed patrons.
- Bo Bardi described the open plaza as an expression of her belief that architecture should serve democratic social purposes rather than private institutional ones.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Bo Bardi's MASP design departed from conventional museum architecture by suspending the exhibition hall above a ground-level plaza that she kept open for all residents, a decision she described as rooted in democratic social purpose."},{"key":"B","text":"The São Paulo Museum of Art, which was completed in 1968, is notable for its striking structural design: two massive concrete beams suspend the entire main exhibition hall above the ground, creating a dramatic visual effect that distinguishes the building from other museums of that period."},{"key":"C","text":"Lina Bo Bardi, an Italian-born architect who emigrated to Brazil in 1946, designed several major public buildings during her career, including the São Paulo Museum of Art, which features an open plaza beneath its elevated exhibition hall."},{"key":"D","text":"Unlike most art museums of the mid-twentieth century, which enclosed their ground floors with walls and limited access to ticketed visitors, the São Paulo Museum of Art features a structurally innovative design that suspends its exhibition hall on two massive concrete beams above an openly accessible civic space."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'The correct choice is the sentence describing Bo Bardi''s decision to keep the plaza open for all residents and her characterization of it as rooted in democratic social purpose, because it directly connects a specific design feature to the philosophy of architecture as public service stated in the claim. The sentence focusing on the two massive concrete beams and the dramatic visual effect emphasizes structural and aesthetic innovation rather than the social philosophy behind the design. The sentence introducing Bo Bardi''s biography and listing the open plaza as a feature provides background information but does not explain how the design reflects a philosophy of public service — it merely describes without connecting the design to a purpose. The sentence contrasting the MASP with enclosed museums and highlighting the structural suspension describes how the building differs architecturally from its contemporaries but stops short of connecting that difference to Bo Bardi''s stated belief that architecture should serve democratic ends, addressing only part of the claim without completing the philosophical link.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 105, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-10-11"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-10-11","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);

